### What is happens-before?

Happens-before is a formal guarantee defined by the Java Memory Model. If action A happens-before action B, then every write performed by A (and all writes that happen-before A, transitively) is visible to B. Without this guarantee, the JVM makes no promises about cross-thread visibility. A thread can read a stale value from its CPU cache and never see the updated value written by another thread.

### Why do threads read stale values without happens-before?

Modern CPUs have per-core caches (L1, L2) and store buffers. When a thread writes to a variable, the write may sit in the store buffer or L1 cache and never be flushed to main memory. Another thread reading the same variable on a different core reads from its own cache, which still holds the old value. The JIT compiler makes this worse by hoisting variable reads out of loops and caching them in a CPU register.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class StaleReadDemo {
    private static boolean configLoaded = false;  // NOT volatile
    private static String interestRate = "0";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> {
            interestRate = "7.5";
            configLoaded = true;  // no happens-before!
        }, "ConfigLoader").start();

        new Thread(() -> {
            while (!configLoaded) {
                // JIT may hoist the read of configLoaded out of the loop
                // into a register. Thread spins forever seeing false.
            }
            // Even if we get here, interestRate might still be "0"
            // because there is no happens-before for interestRate either.
            System.out.println("Rate: " + interestRate);
        }, "Worker").start();
    }
}
```

### What are all seven happens-before rules?

Rule 1: Program Order. Within a single thread, each statement happens-before the next statement in program order. This ensures that single-threaded behavior is predictable, even though the JIT may reorder instructions that have no data dependency.

Rule 2: Monitor Lock. An unlock on a monitor happens-before every subsequent lock on that same monitor. When Thread-1 exits a synchronized block and Thread-2 enters a synchronized block on the same lock, Thread-2 sees all writes made by Thread-1 before the unlock.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MonitorHBDemo {
    private static BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;
    private static final Object lock = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread writer = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock) {
                balance = new BigDecimal("50000.00");  // write inside sync
            }  // UNLOCK: happens-before subsequent lock
        });

        writer.start();
        writer.join();

        synchronized (lock) {  // LOCK: sees all writes before previous unlock
            System.out.println("Balance: " + balance);  // guaranteed 50000.00
        }
    }
}
```

Rule 3: Volatile. A write to a volatile variable happens-before every subsequent read of that same volatile variable. This is the foundation of the volatile flag pattern.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class VolatileHBDemo {
    private static volatile boolean ready = false;
    private static int data = 0;  // NOT volatile

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> {
            data = 42;          // non-volatile write
            ready = true;       // volatile WRITE: happens-before
        }).start();

        new Thread(() -> {
            if (ready) {        // volatile READ: sees the write
                // By TRANSITIVITY: data write HB ready write HB ready read
                System.out.println(data);  // guaranteed 42
            }
        }).start();
    }
}
```

Rule 4: Thread.start(). Calling `thread.start()` happens-before any action in the started thread. All writes before start() are visible to the new thread.

Rule 5: Thread.join(). All actions in a thread happen-before join() returns in the calling thread. After join(), all writes from the completed thread are visible.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class StartJoinHBDemo {
    private static int result = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        result = 10;  // write before start()
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            // Rule 4: sees result = 10 (start() HB thread body)
            result = result + 32;
        });
        worker.start();
        worker.join();
        // Rule 5: sees result = 42 (thread body HB join return)
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);  // guaranteed 42
    }
}
```

Rule 6: Final Fields. The end of a constructor happens-before the freeze of final fields. Any thread that sees a properly published reference to the object will see the fully initialized final fields.

Rule 7: Transitivity. If A happens-before B, and B happens-before C, then A happens-before C. This is the most powerful rule because it chains visibility across multiple operations.

### How does the volatile flag pattern use transitivity?

The volatile flag pattern is the most common application of transitivity. You write non-volatile data fields, then write a volatile flag. A reader checks the volatile flag, and if it sees the write, transitivity guarantees that all prior non-volatile writes are also visible.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class VolatileFlagPattern {
    private BigDecimal balance;    // NOT volatile
    private String accountName;    // NOT volatile
    private volatile boolean published = false;  // volatile flag

    public void initialize() {
        balance = new BigDecimal("75000.00");   // write 1
        accountName = "Rajesh Savings SAV2001";  // write 2
        published = true;  // volatile write: HB edge
        // Transitivity: write1 HB write2 HB volatile-write
    }

    public void read() {
        if (published) {  // volatile read: sees the write
            // Transitivity: volatile-read HB means we see write1 and write2
            System.out.println(accountName + ": " + balance);
            // Guaranteed to see fully initialized values
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with happens-before?

The most common mistake is assuming that volatile on one variable provides visibility for all variables. Volatile only creates a happens-before edge for the specific volatile variable. The visibility of other variables depends on transitivity: they must have been written BEFORE the volatile write in program order.

```java
// WRONG: writing non-volatile AFTER volatile does not help
volatile boolean flag = false;
int data = 0;

// Thread-1:
flag = true;  // volatile write
data = 42;    // AFTER volatile: NOT covered by happens-before!

// Thread-2:
if (flag) {
    // data may still be 0! The write to data is AFTER the volatile write
    // so transitivity does NOT apply.
}

// CORRECT: write non-volatile BEFORE volatile
data = 42;    // BEFORE volatile write
flag = true;  // volatile write creates HB edge covering data write
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers should understand that happens-before is a partial order, not a total order. Two actions from different threads that have no happens-before relationship between them can appear in any order to any observer. The JMM does not define a global clock or a single timeline of events across threads. This means that reasoning about concurrent programs requires identifying the happens-before edges explicitly, not relying on intuition about timing.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does happens-before mean the action physically happens before? No. It is a visibility guarantee, not a temporal ordering. The JIT may execute the instructions in any order as long as the happens-before contract is preserved for other threads.
- What happens if you read without a happens-before edge? The JMM permits any value that was ever written to the variable, or the default value, to be returned.
- Is happens-before transitive across different volatile variables? No. Transitivity only applies along the happens-before chain of the same edges. Writing volatile-A and reading volatile-B creates no relationship unless there is a chain connecting them.
